  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 1212 PM MST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nA ridge axis remains just to the west of our forecast area today,\nwith weak flow aloft. Unsurprisingly, this has led to another day\nof above normal temperatures and sunny skies across the region. We\nshould see highs reach the mid to upper 60s across the Denver\nmetro and eastern plains. \n\nThe upper ridge should begin to weaken tonight into Friday, as a\nweak shortwave approaches. We'll still see above normal\ntemperatures during the day, but increasing cloud cover should\nhold temperatures in the low to mid 60s.\n\nThere's still a surprising amount of uncertainty with this\nweekend's forecast, as a shortwave (or the remnants of a more\norganized cutoff low) pushes southeastward from the Pacific\nnorthwest into southern Colorado by Saturday night. This will\nusher in some cooler air (high temperatures near normal), along\nwith a gradual increase in moisture. What remains to be seen is if\nwe manage to get any organized precipitation in our forecast area.\nAcross the mountains, sufficiently steep lapse rates would favor\nwidely scattered rain and snow showers initially on Saturday,\nbefore transitioning to scattered light orographic snow showers \nSaturday night as the flow aloft turns to the west. Across the \nI-25 corridor, it looks like it will be challenging to saturate \nthe column below 700mb given a lack of appreciable synoptic scale \nascent/upslope flow. However, some moisture could stream into the \neastern plains where guidance generally advertises some light \n(~0.1\") rainfall totals through Sunday AM.\n\nThat system should quickly depart the region on Sunday, leading to\na cool but quiet end to the weekend. Temperatures should again\nremain in the low 50s for most of the plains. A slight warming\ntrend is likely for the start of next week as broad ridging\ndevelops over the central CONUS, leaving most of the state in a\ndrier southwesterly flow aloft. \n\nThe forecast pattern becomes much more complicated by mid-week\nnext week. A series of strong upper troughs is expected to push\nfrom the northern Pacific into the intermountain west during this\nperiod. The first of these waves looks to arrive sometime by \nTuesday or Wednesday. At this time, the best chance of \nprecipitation looks to be over our northern mountains, with \ndecreasing chances off to the south and east. I will emphasize \nthat this type of forecast pattern is volatile, meaning that \nforecast confidence is rather low. Readers are encouraged not to \nfocus on single, deterministic model solutions for the next few \ndays.",
